5. INDEXES — focus on PARTIAL indexes (#1 suspected drift)
================================================================================
Partial indexes (WHERE ...):  UAT = 47   baseline = 30   -> 17 MISSING in baseline.
By predicate:
  WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)     : UAT 41 | baseline 25   (16 missing)
  WHERE (is_preferred = true)    : UAT  1 | baseline  0   ( 1 missing)
  WHERE (deleted_by IS NULL)     : UAT  4 | baseline  4   (match)
  WHERE ((item_type IS NULL) AND (deleted_by IS NULL)) : UAT 1 | baseline 1 (match)

The 17 missing partial indexes (table (cols) WHERE pred) — all NON-UNIQUE (performance):
  application."user" (department_id)                         WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL) *
  mes.part_level (code)                                      WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  mes.part_level (is_active)                                 WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  mes.part_level (sort_order)                                WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  mes.subsystem (code)                                       WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  mes.subsystem (is_active)                                  WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.resource_allocation (project_id)                        WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.resource_allocation (start_date, end_date)              WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.resource_allocation (user_id)                           WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.resource_allocation (user_id, start_date, end_date)     WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.task_dependency (predecessor_task_id)                   WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  pm.task_dependency (successor_task_id)                     WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  sc.company_part (is_preferred)                             WHERE (is_preferred = true)
  sc.purchase_order (department_id)                          WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL) *
  sc.requisition (department_id)                             WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL) *
  sc.stock_movement_line_item (part_id)                      WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)
  sc.stock_movement_line_item (stock_movement_id)            WHERE (deleted_at IS NULL)

  (*) baseline DOES create a plain (non-partial) FK-backing btree index on these
      `department_id` columns, but it lacks the `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL` predicate,
      so it is structurally different from UAT's partial index.

UNIQUE partial indexes: UAT 2 | baseline 2 — MATCH (no uniqueness/correctness drift).

Other index notes:
- baseline emits 287 CREATE INDEX vs UAT 77 — baseline auto-creates an index for every
  FK (EF convention). This is ADDITIVE and COSMETIC (extra perf indexes, no semantic loss).
- 4 UAT indexes carry `WITH (fillfactor='100', deduplicate_items='true')` storage params
  that baseline omits — COSMETIC (storage tuning, not schema shape).

